 # Managed killable rwsem mechanism fixture
 
-This fixture exercises private typed rwsem acquisition and cancellation. It compiled and signed for kernel8 from snapshot `140aecc4`. The earlier runner passed the VM syntax check; the named-span correction remains source-reviewed only. An initial attempt completed the three cold cases, then failed at source-span selection before constructing an image. Generated-case validation remains pending. This fixture does not execute mmap or replace real syscall validation. It binds with `source_calls=false`, so its ordinary Linux killable calls remain callable/native. The separate global source opt-in is not used here. No performance, full C-function generated return, arbitrary nested killable wait or general support claim follows from the build. [Affected build evidence](../../evidence/kernel8-affected-build-20261111/README.md)
+This fixture exercises private typed rwsem acquisition and cancellation. It compiled and signed for kernel8 from snapshot `140aecc4`. An initial attempt completed the three cold cases, then failed at source-span selection before constructing an image. Its separate ordinary recovery completed. A second attempt using the corrected named spans published an image and reached the first learning scene, then timed out waiting for W's actual exit while the fixture withheld C's exit ACK. That attempt remains `failed_retained`, with cleanup pending. The grouped exit-ACK correction below is source-only and unvalidated. Complete generated-case validation remains pending. This fixture does not execute mmap or replace real syscall validation. It binds with `source_calls=false`, so its ordinary Linux killable calls remain callable/native. The separate global source opt-in is not used here. No performance, full C-function generated return, arbitrary nested killable wait or general support claim follows from the build. [Affected build evidence](../../evidence/kernel8-affected-build-20261111/README.md), [initial failure and recovery](../../evidence/kernel8-killable-region-failure-20261111/README.md)
 
 `payload/fixture.c` binds a private Linux rwsem to one KD gate and a cold finite native pool with `source_calls=false`. Native H/R holders use ordinary callable Linux APIs. Managed child RUN ioctls enroll the actual calling task before invoking the private source body. No kernel worker impersonates the original requester. The child returns through its own native continuation and does not explicitly leave the runtime. RUN remains stopped after body return until a parent exit ACK. That gate retains the actual current endpoint for metadata snapshots. The original task clears its raw storage observer before returning to userspace. Its actual process exit then exercises the managed exit hook.
 
 The runner uses real `fork`, deliberate scene-specific `SIGKILL`, and bounded `waitpid(WNOHANG)` observations. A kernel `done` flag is never a task-exit witness. Signals are sent only after the required actual queue/control predicate. Deadlines report failure and retain the controller, transport and original actors. A failure does not release gates, kill remaining actors, unload, settle records or replay a body. Control devices precede composition and report `setup_error` plus exact retained roles. RUN stays closed on any construction error. A failed constructor with a non-NULL runtime remains queryable without a guessed destroy. Clean NULL failures use checked ordinary unwind, and an unwind refusal preserves every remaining role for RETIRE. Later recovery requires its own reviewed ordinary cleanup.
 
+Proxy learning and completion scenes separate exit permission from actual exit. After W's native helper is released, every requested C/W participant, and D when present, must report its expected API result, once-only effects and helper return. The runner joins W's exact synchronous release request, handling, physical completion and typed native unlock. It saves all participant status and the immutable raw event prefix before any exit ACK. It then acknowledges every completed participant before waiting for either task to exit. W's physical batch is stored in W's slot but retired by C, so holding C at the fixture barrier while waiting for W's storage teardown would create a controller-induced wait cycle. Each ACK is tracked by exact role and child PID, duplicate or mismatched ACKs are refused, and the record is cleared only after actual reaping. All actual exit dispositions, owner/slot retirement, final scope counts and drain checks remain required. The cold queued-exit case keeps its original single-role ACK and actual W exit before H is released.
+
 The cases cover the following distinct outcomes.
 
 - An immediately available root grant wins over an already pending fatal signal, returns zero, and performs its body and helper effects once.
